\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Equilibrium CO$_2$ mole fraction in the aqueous phase ($x_{\text  {CO}_2}$, mol\%) over the $T$--$P$ plane, computed from the eCPA ternary flash for six NaCl molalities (columns, $m_s \approx 0$--$6$\,mol\,kg$^{-1}$) and three overall CO$_2$ feed fractions (rows, $z \approx 0.09$, 0.51, 0.90). The logarithmic colour scale spans three orders of magnitude to resolve both the dilute near-bubble-point regime and the high-solubility hydrothermal limit. Grey regions are thermodynamically single-phase at those conditions. Red curves mark the phase envelope: the solid line is the dew-point boundary (lower pressure limit of the two-phase window) and the dashed line is the bubble-point boundary (upper pressure limit). The systematic leftward shift of the bubble-point curve with increasing $m_s$ (columns) quantifies the salting-out effect over the full $T$--$P$ domain.}}{23}{figure.caption.26}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ecpa_aq_grid}{{9}{23}{Equilibrium CO$_2$ mole fraction in the aqueous phase ($x_{\text {CO}_2}$, mol\%) over the $T$--$P$ plane, computed from the eCPA ternary flash for six NaCl molalities (columns, $m_s \approx 0$--$6$\,mol\,kg$^{-1}$) and three overall CO$_2$ feed fractions (rows, $z \approx 0.09$, 0.51, 0.90). The logarithmic colour scale spans three orders of magnitude to resolve both the dilute near-bubble-point regime and the high-solubility hydrothermal limit. Grey regions are thermodynamically single-phase at those conditions. Red curves mark the phase envelope: the solid line is the dew-point boundary (lower pressure limit of the two-phase window) and the dashed line is the bubble-point boundary (upper pressure limit). The systematic leftward shift of the bubble-point curve with increasing $m_s$ (columns) quantifies the salting-out effect over the full $T$--$P$ domain}{figure.caption.26}{}}
